verification.reason:
Confirmed at b6fca69. I read the contract myself. web/client.js:689-692 says: "Its yes is true, or, where `rehearsalNeedsPassphrase` says so, `{ passphrase }` ...; anything else stops the rekey with NOT_CONFIRMED_BY_OWNER". The answerOwner comment (:1361-1363) says "Anything else is a no". docs/BROWSER-PACKAGE.md:290-291 says the library "seals nothing before that yes". scripts/package-check/client-rekey.mjs:66 says "any other answer is a no". The Rust binding (src/wasm_api/core/rekey.rs:333) is strict on purpose: "Only the value true is a yes". CHECK-API-001 requires extra fields to be rejected "rather than guessing another valid mode". CORE-48 says two different inputs never map silently to the same meaning. The specification (mhfe_spec README.md:518-519) says the application "waits for the owner's explicit confirmation and MUST NOT proceed without that confirmation".

answerOwner (web/client.js:1368-1378) reads `confirmed` only from whether `passphrase` is present. My own probe uses the real dist/core/client.js with a stand-in rekey worker and runs no Argon2. Four answers reached the worker as confirmed: true:
- { confirmed: false, passphrase }
- { ok: false, passphrase }
- { passphrase, cancelled: true }
- a Promise of the first

The documented shapes behave as documented. For contrast, the same class refuses extra keys on confirmations and references.

One part of the candidate is wrong and I corrected it: TypeScript does not catch this. tsc 7.0.2 with the strict flags, run against dist/core/client.d.ts, gives no error for an owner arrow function (sync or async) that returns { confirmed, passphrase }, or for one that returns a variable holding it. It flags only the control cases. So typed pages are exposed as well. Severity stays medium under AUDIT_STANDARD.md: an API-contract defect with no demonstrated direct secret compromise, which needs an off-contract answer shape from the page. It is related to AUD-023-API001 (extra keys silently accepted, fixed only in describeReference) but has its own root cause. It is not a duplicate of the wave finding about a dropped passphrase on a built-in-check yes, which is a different defect with a different fix.

